{
  "summary": "The os.execv self-restart approach has several critical flaws that the previous proposal glossed over. First, os.execv does NOT run atexit handlers — it replaces the process image immediately — so the TUI's lock file (acquired via _acquire_lock, released via atexit.register) will never be released, causing the restarted process to fail lock acquisition and refuse to start. Second, os.execv does not cleanly shut down Textual's terminal state (alternate screen buffer, raw mode, mouse capture), so the terminal will be left corrupted before the new process starts, producing visual artifacts or requiring the user to run 'reset'. Third, the proposal underestimates the UX disruption: a full restart tears down the websocket server connection, loses all scroll positions, closes any open modals (there are 8+ modal screen classes), and produces a visible flash — this is far more jarring than 'picking up code changes' warrants. A more practical approach would be to skip runtime code reload entirely and instead focus on what actually matters: the TUI's behavior is data-driven (it reads JSON feature files from disk), and the existing _check_for_changes polling already handles data refresh. The only scenario where Python code changes matter is during active development of the TUI itself, which is better served by simply restarting the TUI manually (Ctrl+C and re-run) — developers already do this. If automatic restart is truly needed for developer convenience, the supervisor/wrapper pattern (Approach 1) is superior because it can cleanly terminate the child TUI process (allowing proper Textual shutdown and lock release), wait for terminal reset, then spawn a fresh process, with debouncing built in. The os.execv approach is a trap: it looks simple but the interaction with locks, terminal state, and Textual's lifecycle makes it unreliable in practice."
}
